        "# Cryptoeconomic Game Theory Simulator\n",
        "\n",
        "A Python-based mechanism design simulator that models validator behavior in Proof-of-Stake (PoS) networks using a 2-player Nash Equilibrium framework.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Overview\n",
        "\n",
        "In decentralized networks, cryptoeconomic security relies on ensuring that honest participation is a dominant strategy. This simulator models the decision-making process of two validators deciding whether to **Stake** (act honestly) or **Defect** (equivocate, extract malicious MEV, or collude).\n",
        "\n",
        "The simulator calculates pure and mixed strategy Nash Equilibria and generates a Matplotlib heatmap reflecting the resulting social welfare of the network.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Payoff Matrix Model\n",
        "\n",
        "The mechanism is formalized as a normal-form game:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P = \\begin{bmatrix} (R, R) & (M, B) \\\\ (B, M) & (-S, -S) \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "* **$R$ (Reward):** Base network issuance for honest validation.\n",
        "* **$B$ (Bribe/MEV):** Short-term illicit profit from defecting against an honest validator.\n",
        "* **$M$ (Missed):** Payoff when acting honestly while the counterpart defects.\n",
        "* **$-S$ (Slashing):** Cryptoeconomic penalty applied when both validators defect (network halts/slashing conditions met).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Features\n",
        "- **Nash Equilibrium Calculation:** Uses the `nashpy` library to solve for both pure and mixed strategies.\n",
        "- **Welfare Heatmap:** Generates an annotated 2x2 `matplotlib` visualization of the matrix, colored by the total social welfare of the resulting state.\n",
        "- **Parameterizable Penalties:** Easily adjust variables to test how severe a slashing penalty must be to offset high MEV bribes.\n",
        "\n",
      
      }
    }
